-- title:
--   A ℤ_{(ℓ)}-point of the Igusa pole chart
-- statement:
--   Let $N$ be a natural number, nonzero, and let $\ell$ be a prime. Write $\mathbb{Z}_{(\ell)}$ for the subring [`GaloisRep.ratLocalizedAt ℓ`](def/GaloisRep_Flat.html#L8) of $\mathbb{Q}$ consisting of those rationals whose denominator (in lowest terms) is coprime to $\ell$, and let $F$ denote the full modular function field of level $N$, `modularFunctionFieldFull N`, with $j \in F$ the element `jFull N` given by the $q$-series $j$. The pole chart algebra `chartAlgInf N ℓ` is by definition `chartAlg N ℓ {(jFull N)⁻¹}`, the $\mathbb{Z}_{(\ell)}$-subalgebra of $F$ whose elements are those $x \in F$ that are integral over the subring $\mathbb{Z}_{(\ell)}[j^{-1}]$ generated by $\mathbb{Z}_{(\ell)}$ and $j^{-1}$ inside $F$; that is, the integral closure of $\mathbb{Z}_{(\ell)}[1/j]$ in $F$. The assertion is that the type of $\mathbb{Z}_{(\ell)}$-algebra homomorphisms from `chartAlgInf N ℓ` to $\mathbb{Z}_{(\ell)}$ is nonempty: there exists at least one ring homomorphism $\varepsilon \colon \mathbb{Z}_{(\ell)}[1/j]^{\mathrm{int}} \to \mathbb{Z}_{(\ell)}$ compatible with the $\mathbb{Z}_{(\ell)}$-structures, hence restricting to the identity on $\mathbb{Z}_{(\ell)}$. No particular such homomorphism is singled out, and no uniqueness is claimed.
--
--   Geometrically this says that $\operatorname{Spec}$ of the pole chart of the Igusa scheme at level $N$ has a $\mathbb{Z}_{(\ell)}$-valued point, the expected one being the cusp at infinity, where $1/j$ vanishes and the retraction is the constant term of the $q$-expansion. It is the algebraic input to [`ModularCurve.IgusaScheme.nonempty_schemeHomOver_id_igusaTo`](thm.html#ModularCurve.IgusaScheme.nonempty_schemeHomOver_id_igusaTo), which produces a section of the Igusa scheme over $\operatorname{Spec} \mathbb{Z}_{(\ell)}$.
